D['ovarian-cancer']=dict(searches=5,secs=[
("What are the symptoms of ovarian cancer?",[O+'symptoms/','https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/ovarian-cancer','https://www.royalmarsden.nhs.uk/private-care/news-and-blogs/signs-look-out-ovarian-cancer'],
"The NHS lists a swollen tummy or feeling bloated as a main symptom, and says symptoms are the ones that happen frequently, roughly 12 or more times a month. Cancer Research UK adds a swollen or painful tummy, loss of appetite and changes in passing urine. Early-stage disease may cause no symptoms, and any that do occur tend to be very general.\n\nThe Royal Marsden notes that many of these symptoms overlap with other conditions, such as irritable bowel syndrome and endometriosis. That is one reason a symptom on its own does not mean cancer.\n\nThe NHS advises seeing a GP about symptoms like these that are new, persistent or frequent for the person. A GP appointment is the usual first step, and the GP decides whether tests such as a CA125 blood test are needed."),
("Who is at risk of ovarian cancer?",[O+'causes/','https://www.royalmarsden.nhs.uk/your-care/cancer-types/gynaecological/ovarian-cancer'],
"The NHS says the risk of ovarian cancer increases with age, and most cases in the UK are in women over 50. It lists an inherited faulty gene, such as the BRCA genes or those linked to Lynch syndrome, a previous breast or bowel cancer, previous radiotherapy, endometriosis, smoking and being overweight among the factors that raise risk.\n\nThe Royal Marsden notes that BRCA1 and BRCA2 are two genes known to be associated with an increased risk of both breast and ovarian cancer. Taking the combined contraceptive pill appears to reduce the risk. The same source says there is no concrete evidence that fertility treatment increases it.\n\nRisk factors do not guarantee disease. Many people with several risk factors never develop ovarian cancer, and some people with none do."),
("How are the stage and type of ovarian cancer described?",['https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/ovarian-cancer','https://ovarian.org.uk/ovarian-cancer/stages-and-grades-of-ovarian-cancer/','https://targetovariancancer.org.uk/about-ovarian-cancer/treatment/targeted-treatments-ovarian-cancer'],
"Ovarian cancer is staged from 1 to 4. Cancer Research UK describes stage 1 as cancer found only in one or both ovaries, and stage 4 as cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body such as the liver or lungs. Grade describes how abnormal the cells look under the microscope. Stage and grade are used together to plan treatment and to judge the outlook.\n\nThere are different types. Target Ovarian Cancer describes high-grade serous cancer, which is the type PARP inhibitors are mainly used for, and low-grade serous cancer, for which a different group of targeted drugs, MEK inhibitors, are used.\n\nMedlinePlus says survival depends strongly on stage at diagnosis and is better when the cancer is found before it spreads beyond the ovary. Cancer Research UK and other charities hold current national figures."),
("How is ovarian cancer treated?",[O+'treatment/','https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/ovarian-cancer/','https://targetovariancancer.org.uk/about-ovarian-cancer/treatment/targeted-treatments-ovarian-cancer','https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/treatment-for-ovarian-cancer/'],
"The main treatments are surgery and chemotherapy. Surgery is usually the first step. In early ovarian cancer, surgery may be the only treatment needed, and chemotherapy afterwards aims to lower the risk of the cancer returning. In advanced disease, chemotherapy may be given before surgery, after it, or both. NHS inform says chemotherapy is sometimes given on its own as the main treatment if surgery is not possible. Radiotherapy is rarely used and is mainly for easing symptoms.\n\nTarget Ovarian Cancer describes targeted treatments. PARP inhibitors are offered to people with stage 3 or 4 high-grade epithelial ovarian, fallopian tube or primary peritoneal cancer that has responded to platinum-based chemotherapy, and can usually only be accessed once. Bevacizumab is available whether or not someone has a BRCA variant.\n\nThe Royal Marsden notes that people with the same type of cancer may have different treatment plans, which a specialist team agrees with them."),
("Life after treatment for ovarian cancer",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/ovarian-cancer/','https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/treatment-for-ovarian-cancer/'],
"NHS inform says that anyone who has not yet been through the menopause will usually be brought into it by treatment. Cancer and its treatment can cause physical and emotional changes that affect a person's sex life, and difficulties usually slowly improve after treatment.\n\nFollow-up involves regular check-ups. CA125 blood tests are sometimes done as part of follow-up, and a rising level may be a sign that the cancer has returned. Ovarian cancer can come back, and a person who notices new symptoms, or symptoms they had before returning, is advised to contact their specialist doctor or nurse.\n\nSpecialist nurses can offer support, and Macmillan Cancer Support has information on coping with advanced cancer. The NHS says a person with ovarian cancer can get support from their specialist team and from charities.")
],faqs=[
("How is ovarian cancer diagnosed?","A blood test and a scan are usually the first tests. CA125 is a blood marker that is often raised in ovarian cancer, and imaging is typically an ultrasound or CT scan. Tissue from a biopsy or from surgery is examined under a microscope to confirm the diagnosis.",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/ovarian-cancer/','https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/ovarian-cancer']),
("Do bloating and tummy pain mean ovarian cancer?","Not usually. The Royal Marsden notes these symptoms overlap with other conditions such as irritable bowel syndrome and endometriosis. The NHS advises seeing a GP if they are new, persistent or frequent, so the cause can be looked into.",['https://www.royalmarsden.nhs.uk/your-care/cancer-types/gynaecological/ovarian-cancer',O+'symptoms/']),
("Will treatment bring on the menopause?","NHS inform says that if the person has not already been through the menopause, treatment will usually cause it. The specialist team can explain the effects and the options for managing them.",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/ovarian-cancer/']),
("What are PARP inhibitors?","They are targeted drugs. Target Ovarian Cancer says they are offered to people with stage 3 or 4 high-grade epithelial ovarian, fallopian tube or primary peritoneal cancer that has responded to platinum-based chemotherapy, and can usually only be accessed once.",['https://targetovariancancer.org.uk/about-ovarian-cancer/treatment/targeted-treatments-ovarian-cancer']),
("Can ovarian cancer come back?","Yes. NHS inform says ovarian cancer can return, and follow-up check-ups look out for this. A person who notices new symptoms, or old ones returning, is advised to contact their specialist doctor or nurse.",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/ovarian-cancer/']),
],facts=[
("Main symptom","A swollen tummy or bloating that is frequent or persistent"),
("First tests","A CA125 blood test and a scan, usually ultrasound or CT"),
("Confirmation","Tissue examined under a microscope after a biopsy or surgery"),
("Stages","1 to 4, from cancer in the ovaries to spread to distant organs"),
("Main treatments","Surgery and chemotherapy, with targeted drugs in some cases"),
("Route to care","A GP appointment for new, persistent or frequent symptoms"),
],refs=[("NHS: Ovarian cancer symptoms","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ovarian-cancer/symptoms/"),("NHS: Ovarian cancer causes","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ovarian-cancer/causes/"),("NHS: Ovarian cancer treatment","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ovarian-cancer/treatment/"),("NHS inform: Ovarian cancer","https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/ovarian-cancer/"),("Target Ovarian Cancer: Targeted treatments","https://targetovariancancer.org.uk/about-ovarian-cancer/treatment/targeted-treatments-ovarian-cancer")])

C='https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/cervical-cancer/'
CR='https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/cervical-cancer'
D['cervical-cancer']=dict(searches=5,secs=[
("What are the symptoms of cervical cancer?",[C+'symptoms/','https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/cervical-cancer/'],
"The NHS lists unusual vaginal bleeding, including bleeding during or after sex, between periods or after the menopause, as the main symptom. Other symptoms are changes in vaginal discharge, pain during sex, and pelvic or lower back pain.\n\nEarly cervical cancer often causes no symptoms, so the NHS says that symptoms appearing between regular screening appointments should not wait for the next one. A GP appointment is the route: the NHS advises being checked if symptoms change, get worse or do not feel normal for the person.\n\nCancer Research UK notes that these symptoms are common and have many other causes, so they do not confirm cancer. Screening cannot assess symptoms. The usual next steps are an examination and, if needed, referral for a closer look at the cervix."),
("What causes cervical cancer?",['https://111.wales.nhs.uk/Encyclopaedia/c/article/cancerofthecervix/','https://www.newcastle-hospitals.nhs.uk/resources/information-for-people-referred-with-a-cervical-sample-smear-abnormality/',C],
"NHS 111 Wales says nearly all cervical cancers are linked to infection with certain high-risk types of the human papillomavirus (HPV). A Newcastle hospitals leaflet adds that HPV is very common and that most people clear the virus without problems.\n\nTwo measures target this chain of events. All children aged 12 to 13 are offered the HPV vaccine, and everyone with a cervix aged 25 to 64 is invited for regular cervical screening. This includes women and anyone else who has a cervix. Screening looks for HPV and abnormal cells before cancer develops.\n\nScreening is a way of finding changes early. It is not a diagnostic test for cancer, and one source notes that Pap smears screen for, but do not diagnose, cervical pre-cancers."),
("How is cervical cancer diagnosed?",['https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/colposcopy/','https://www.nhsinform.scot/tests-and-treatments/non-surgical-procedures/colposcopy/','https://www.royalmarsden.nhs.uk/private-care/news-and-blogs/cervical-cancer-stages-and-diagnosis'],
"Diagnosis often begins with an abnormal screening result. Colposcopy is usually the first step after one: the cervix is looked at closely, and a small punch biopsy is common. A loop excision (LLETZ) or cone biopsy can also give tissue to examine. Colposcopy does not establish a diagnosis of cancer on its own. A biopsy does.\n\nNHS inform notes that cancer is found in only a very small share of the people referred for colposcopy.\n\nIf cancer is found, tests such as an MRI scan, a PET-CT scan or a chest X-ray may be used to see how far it has spread. Doctors describe this as the stage, using the FIGO system, which has four stages numbered 1 to 4 based on the size of the cancer and whether it has spread."),
("How is cervical cancer treated?",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/cervical-cancer/','https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/cervical-cancer/treatment/chemotherapy-treatment','https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/cervical-cancer/stages-types-grades/stage-4','https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/cervical-cancer/stages-types-grades/stage-2'],
"Treatment usually includes surgery, chemotherapy or radiotherapy, and depends on the size and type of the cancer and where it is. Surgery is the main treatment for early disease, and the types include LLETZ, cone biopsy, hysterectomy and trachelectomy. Radiotherapy may be used instead of surgery for someone not fit for a big operation or general anaesthetic, or after surgery to reduce the risk of the cancer coming back.\n\nCancer Research UK says chemotherapy can be given at the same time as radiotherapy, called chemoradiotherapy, for stage 1B2 to stage 4A cervical cancer, and that cisplatin is the most common drug. For stage 2, chemoradiotherapy is the main treatment, sometimes with surgery. In stage 4A a few people may be offered a pelvic exenteration, a major operation.\n\nFor cancer that has spread, treatment can include the immunotherapy drug pembrolizumab with chemotherapy, with or without the targeted drug bevacizumab. Small cell cancer of the cervix is rare and is treated differently."),
("Fertility and support",[C,'https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/cervical-cancer/'],
"The specialist care team will talk about the effect treatment may have on fertility. If fertility cannot be preserved through the treatment itself, a doctor can refer the person to a fertility specialist. Options such as storing eggs or embryos before treatment can be discussed. Trachelectomy is one operation designed for people who wish to keep the option of pregnancy.\n\nThe NHS says people with cervical cancer can get support from their specialist team and from charities such as Macmillan Cancer Support. Cancer Research UK notes that coping with a cancer diagnosis can be difficult, both practically and emotionally.\n\nPrognosis depends on the type of cancer, its stage and whether it comes back after treatment. MedlinePlus says the five-year survival rate falls as the cancer spreads outside the walls of the cervix into other areas, which is why finding and treating it early matters.")
],faqs=[
("Does an abnormal screening result mean cancer?","No. An abnormal result leads to colposcopy, a closer look at the cervix. NHS inform notes that cancer is found in only a very small share of the people referred. Most abnormal results lead to treatment of cell changes, not cancer.",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/tests-and-treatments/non-surgical-procedures/colposcopy/']),
("What happens at colposcopy?","The cervix is examined closely, and a small punch biopsy is common. Tissue can also be taken with a loop excision (LLETZ) or cone biopsy. Colposcopy is usually the first step after an abnormal screening result.",['https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/colposcopy/']),
("Does bleeding after sex mean cervical cancer?","Not necessarily. Cancer Research UK notes that these symptoms are common and have many other causes. The NHS still advises seeing a GP about unusual bleeding, because it needs to be checked and cannot be assessed by screening.",[C+'symptoms/',CR]),
("Can I still have children after treatment?","It depends on the treatment. The NHS says the specialist team will talk about the effect on fertility, and can refer to a fertility specialist. Egg or embryo storage before treatment can be discussed, and trachelectomy is an operation that aims to preserve fertility.",[C]),
("Who is invited for cervical screening?","The NHS says everyone with a cervix aged 25 to 64 is invited for regular screening. All children aged 12 to 13 are offered the HPV vaccine.",[C]),
],facts=[
("Main cause","Persistent infection with high-risk types of HPV"),
("Early disease","Often no symptoms"),
("Main symptom","Unusual vaginal bleeding, for example after sex or after the menopause"),
("Key investigation","Colposcopy with a biopsy"),
("Staging","FIGO system, stages 1 to 4"),
("Route to care","A GP appointment for unusual bleeding or other symptoms; do not wait for the next screening invitation"),
],refs=[("NHS: Symptoms of cervical cancer","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/cervical-cancer/symptoms/"),("NHS inform: Cervical cancer","https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer/cancer-types-in-adults/cervical-cancer/"),("Cancer Research UK: Cervical cancer","https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/cervical-cancer"),("Cancer Research UK: Chemotherapy for cervical cancer","https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/cervical-cancer/treatment/chemotherapy-treatment"),("NHS inform: Colposcopy","https://www.nhsinform.scot/tests-and-treatments/non-surgical-procedures/colposcopy/")])

P='https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos/'
D['polycystic-ovary-syndrome']=dict(searches=4,secs=[
("What are the symptoms of PCOS?",[P+'diagnosis/','https://www.nhsinform.scot/healthy-living/womens-health/girls-and-young-women-puberty-to-around-25/periods-and-menstrual-health/polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos/','https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/p/article/polycysticovarysyndrome/'],
"Irregular or infrequent periods, extra hair on the face or body (hirsutism) and difficulty getting pregnant are among the most commonly listed signs. Acne, oily skin, weight gain, particularly around the waist, and thinning scalp hair also appear on some lists.\n\nThe NHS says infertility in PCOS is mainly due to a failure of ovulation. NHS 111 Wales lists less common symptoms: tiredness, depression, hair loss, miscarriage, pelvic pain and mood swings.\n\nSymptoms vary a lot. NHS hospital leaflets say some women have mild symptoms, while others are affected more severely by a wider range of symptoms. NHS inform now titles its page on the condition polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS), a name that reflects the hormonal and metabolic features of the condition."),
("How is PCOS diagnosed?",[P+'diagnosis/','https://www.verity-pcos.org.uk/uploads/6/7/0/2/6702442/verity_appg_pcos_report_sept25.pdf'],
"The NHS says a diagnosis can usually be made if other rare causes of the same symptoms have been ruled out and at least two of three criteria are met: irregular or infrequent periods, signs of excess male hormones, and polycystic-looking ovaries on a scan. Diagnosis usually involves hormone and blood tests to rule out other hormone-related conditions, and sometimes an ultrasound scan.\n\nHaving polycystic-looking ovaries on a scan is not enough by itself. A hospital leaflet says that around six or seven in 100 women with polycystic ovaries have PCOS, and that polycystic ovaries by themselves do not need treatment.\n\nDiagnosis can be difficult because signs can come and go. A 2025 report by the all-party parliamentary group on PCOS says many people remain undiagnosed. A GP appointment is the usual first step for anyone with irregular periods or other symptoms."),
("How is PCOS treated?",['https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos/','https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/p/article/polycysticovarysyndrome/','https://mft.nhs.uk/app/uploads/2021/06/PCOS-Gail-Busby-and-Montilla-002.pdf'],
"The NHS says there is no cure for PCOS, but the symptoms can be treated. NHS 111 Wales advises that losing weight and eating a healthy, balanced diet can make some symptoms better for people who are overweight.\n\nA Manchester NHS trust document lists the combined pill as the first-choice treatment for irregular bleeding, with metformin also listed for features such as excess hair and acne. Medicines are also available for excess hair growth and for fertility problems.\n\nThe choice depends on which symptoms matter most to the person, and the GP or specialist advises on it. The same document says that, where body mass index is raised, a weight loss of 5 to 10 per cent is encouraged with referral to a dietitian."),
("PCOS and fertility",[P,'https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/p/article/polycysticovarysyndrome/'],
"PCOS can disrupt ovulation, which can make it harder to conceive. Fertility treatment can help some women to get a regular cycle and improves their chances of pregnancy.\n\nNHS 111 Wales says clomifene is usually the first treatment recommended for women trying to conceive. If medicines do not work, in vitro fertilisation (IVF) may be offered, and a simple surgical procedure called laparoscopic ovarian drilling may be recommended. The NHS says that with treatment, most women with PCOS are able to get pregnant.\n\nPCOS is also linked with a higher risk of pregnancy complications, such as high blood pressure, pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes and miscarriage, according to NHS pathway documents. Anyone planning a pregnancy is advised to talk to their GP or a fertility specialist."),
("PCOS, long-term health and mood",['https://primarycare.northeastlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Pathway-Polycystic-Ovary-Syndrome-10_2024.pdf','https://www.verity-pcos.org.uk/uploads/6/7/0/2/6702442/verity_appg_pcos_report_sept25.pdf','https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/vqvfrlbj/pcos.pdf'],
"A North East London NHS pathway advises counselling about possible long-term complications, which include type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, obstructive sleep apnoea and cancer of the womb lining (endometrial cancer). Infrequent or absent periods are a risk factor for thickening of the womb lining. One NHS leaflet says PCOS does not increase the chance of breast, cervical or ovarian cancer. Getting symptoms under control earlier may help reduce the long-term risks.\n\nMental health matters too. Fertility problems and visible symptoms can cause distress and lower self-esteem. The 2025 all-party group report says mental health should be built into treatment, and that low mood and anxiety are among the effects. NHS pathways say screening for and managing mental health conditions is essential.\n\nMany women manage their symptoms and long-term risks well, often without medication.")
],faqs=[
("Can I get pregnant with PCOS?","Often, yes. The NHS says that with treatment most women with PCOS are able to get pregnant. PCOS can disrupt ovulation, so it can take longer. Clomifene is usually the first treatment, and IVF may be offered if medicines do not work.",[P,'https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/p/article/polycysticovarysyndrome/']),
("Does having polycystic ovaries on a scan mean I have PCOS?","No. A hospital leaflet says only around six or seven in 100 women with polycystic ovaries have PCOS, and polycystic ovaries by themselves do not need treatment. Diagnosis needs at least two of three criteria.",['https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos/diagnosis/']),
("Is there a cure for PCOS?","The NHS says there is no cure, but the symptoms can be treated. Weight loss and a healthy diet can improve some symptoms, and medicines are available for irregular periods, excess hair and fertility problems.",[P]),
("Does PCOS raise the risk of type 2 diabetes?","NHS pathway documents list type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, obstructive sleep apnoea and endometrial cancer as possible long-term complications. Getting symptoms under control earlier may help reduce these risks.",['https://primarycare.northeastlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Pathway-Polycystic-Ovary-Syndrome-10_2024.pdf']),
("Can PCOS affect mental health?","Fertility problems and visible symptoms can cause distress and lower self-esteem. A 2025 all-party parliamentary group report says low mood and anxiety are among the effects, and NHS pathways say screening for and managing mental health conditions is essential.",['https://www.verity-pcos.org.uk/uploads/6/7/0/2/6702442/verity_appg_pcos_report_sept25.pdf']),
],facts=[
("Also called","Polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS), the name NHS inform now uses"),
("Diagnosis","At least two of three criteria, after other causes are ruled out"),
("Cure","None, but symptoms can be treated"),
("First steps in care","Weight loss and a healthy diet where weight is raised"),
("Fertility","Clomifene is usually the first treatment; IVF may follow"),
("Long-term risks","Type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, sleep apnoea, endometrial cancer"),
],refs=[("NHS: Polycystic ovary syndrome diagnosis","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos/diagnosis/"),("NHS inform: Polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS)","https://www.nhsinform.scot/healthy-living/womens-health/girls-and-young-women-puberty-to-around-25/periods-and-menstrual-health/polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos/"),("NHS 111 Wales: Polycystic ovary syndrome","https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/p/article/polycysticovarysyndrome/")])
